<h2>Riemannian Geometry — The Shape of Curved Space</h2>
<p>Riemannian geometry studies smooth manifolds equipped with a Riemannian metric — an inner product on each tangent space that varies smoothly. The metric defines distances, angles, curves of shortest length (geodesics), and curvature (how the manifold bends). It's the mathematical language of general relativity, where spacetime is a 4-dimensional Lorentzian manifold. The field includes the curvature tensor, geodesic flow, Hopf-Rinow theorem, comparison theorems (Rauch, Bishop-Gromov), and the Ricci flow.</p>
<div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"The curvature of space is what we call gravity."</div><div class="attribution">— John Archibald Wheeler (paraphrasing Einstein)</div></div>
<div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div>
<h2>📏 Riemannian Metrics and the Levi-Civita Connection</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Metrics</div><div class="story-title">Riemannian Metrics, Covariant Derivatives, and Parallel Transport</div><div class="story-text">A <strong>Riemannian metric</strong> g on a manifold M is a smooth positive-definite symmetric bilinear form g_p: T_pM × T_pM → ℝ. In coordinates: g = Σ g_{ij} dx^i dx^j. The <strong>Christoffel symbols</strong> Γ^k_{ij} = ½ g^{kl}(∂_i g_{jl} + ∂_j g_{il} − ∂_l g_{ij}) define the <strong>Levi-Civita connection</strong> ∇ — the unique torsion-free metric-compatible connection. The <strong>covariant derivative</strong> ∇_X Y differentiates vector fields: (∇_X Y)^k = X^i ∂_i Y^k + Γ^k_{ij} X^i Y^j. <strong>Parallel transport</strong> along a curve γ(t): a vector field V(t) is parallel if ∇_{γ̇} V = 0. The angle preserved under parallel transport reflects the curvature of the manifold. The <strong>Riemann curvature tensor</strong> R(X,Y)Z = ∇_X∇_Y Z − ∇_Y∇_X Z − ∇_{[X,Y]}Z measures the non-commutativity of covariant derivatives — equivalently, the holonomy around infinitesimal loops.</div><div class="story-moral">The Riemannian metric defines geometry; the Levi-Civita connection tells you how to differentiate and parallel-transport vectors along curves.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div>
<h2>🌍 Geodesics and the Hopf-Rinow Theorem</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Shortest Paths</div><div class="story-title">Geodesics, Exponential Map, and Completeness</div><div class="story-text">A <strong>geodesic</strong> is a curve γ(t) with zero acceleration: ∇_{γ̇} γ̇ = 0, i.e., d²γ^k/dt² + Γ^k_{ij} γ̇^i γ̇^j = 0. Geodesics are locally length-minimizing. The <strong>exponential map</strong> exp_p(v) = γ_v(1) where γ_v is the geodesic with initial velocity v ∈ T_pM. For small enough v, exp_p is a diffeomorphism onto a neighborhood. The <strong>Gauss lemma</strong>: geodesics from p are orthogonal to the geodesic spheres (exp_p images of Euclidean spheres). The <strong>Hopf-Rinow theorem</strong>: for a Riemannian manifold, the following are equivalent: (1) M is complete as a metric space, (2) closed bounded sets are compact, (3) exp_p is defined on all T_pM. Completeness ⇒ any two points can be joined by a minimizing geodesic. The <strong>injectivity radius</strong> inj(p) is the largest r such that exp_p is injective on B_r(0) ⊂ T_pM.</div><div class="story-moral">Geodesics are the straightest possible curves — they locally minimize length. Hopf-Rinow ensures complete manifolds have geodesics between any pair of points.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div>
<h2>🧮 Curvature — Sectional, Ricci, and Scalar</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Curvature</div><div class="story-title">Sectional Curvature, Ricci Curvature, and the Einstein Equations</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>sectional curvature</strong> K(σ) for a 2-plane σ = span{X,Y} is K = ⟨R(X,Y)Y,X⟩ / (|X|²|Y|²−⟨X,Y⟩²). It generalizes Gauss curvature to higher dimensions. Constant sectional curvature spaces: spheres (K=1), Euclidean space (K=0), hyperbolic space (K=−1). The <strong>Ricci curvature</strong> Ric(v) = Σ⟨R(v,e_i)e_i, v⟩ is the average of sectional curvatures over planes containing v. It appears in the Einstein field equations: R_{μν} − ½ R g_{μν} + Λ g_{μν} = (8πG/c⁴) T_{μν}. The <strong>Bishop-Gromov inequality</strong>: if Ric ≥ (n−1)k, then the volume of geodesic balls of radius r satisfies vol(B_r) ≤ vol(B_r^{(k)}) where the RHS is the volume in the model space of constant curvature k. The <strong>Myers theorem</strong>: if Ric ≥ (n−1)k > 0, then M is compact with diameter ≤ π/√k. The <strong>Bonnet-Myers theorem</strong>: positive Ricci curvature forces the manifold to be compact and finite fundamental group.</div><div class="story-moral">Sectional curvature is the 2-Directional curvature; Ricci curvature averages over directions and controls volume growth and diameter.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div>
<h2>🔥 Ricci Flow and Geometric Analysis</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Flow</div><div class="story-title">Ricci Flow — Evolving Metrics Toward Constant Curvature</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Ricci flow</strong> ∂g/∂t = −2 Ric(g) evolves a Riemannian metric in the direction of its Ricci curvature — smoothing irregularities and pushing toward constant curvature. Hamilton (1982): on a 3-manifold with positive Ricci curvature, Ricci flow converges to a constant curvature metric. The <strong>Ricci flow equation</strong> is a nonlinear parabolic PDE — it's the geometric heat equation. <strong>Singularities</strong> occur when curvature blows up (neck pinch, cigar soliton). <strong>Perelman's breakthrough</strong> (2002-3): used Ricci flow with surgery to prove the <strong>Poincaré conjecture</strong> (every simply connected closed 3-manifold is homeomorphic to S³) and the <strong>Geometrization conjecture</strong> (Thurston). Key tools: <strong>L-geodesics</strong> (reduced distance), <strong>entropy functional</strong> (monotonicity), and <strong>canonical neighborhood theorem</strong>. The <strong>Ricci soliton</strong> solution shrinks or expands self-similarly under Ricci flow. The <strong>Yamabe problem</strong> (solved by Trudinger-Aubin-Schoen): every compact Riemannian manifold admits a metric of constant scalar curvature.</div><div class="story-moral">Ricci flow evolves geometry toward uniformity — Perelman used it to classify 3-manifolds and solve the Poincaré conjecture, one of the greatest achievements in mathematics.</div></div>
